      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Filing a roof insurance claim in Utah\u2014whether you are dealing with heavy winter snow loads, severe windstorms along the Wasatch Front, or summer hail\u2014requires a careful, step-by-step approach.",
        "reference_indexes": [
          0
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Step-by-Step Roof Insurance Claim Process"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "1. Document the Damage First",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Take clear, high-resolution photos and videos of the damage from the ground."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Note the exact date and time the severe weather (hail, high winds, or heavy snow/ice) occurred."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Warning: Avoid climbing onto a damaged or slick roof yourself; let a professional handle close-up inspections."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "2. Get a Free Professional Inspection",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Contact a reputable local roofing contractor in Utah that offers insurance claim assistance (such as Chalk Line Roofing or local providers like Definitive Construction) to perform a no-obligation inspection."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Ask the contractor to document missing shingles, bruised/creased tabs from hail, or structural strain.",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  1,
                  2
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "3. Review Your Insurance Policy",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Check your deductible (note that many Utah policies have a separate, higher percentage-based deductible for wind/hail or specific actual cash value [ACV] vs. replacement cost value [RCV] clauses)."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Confirm your provider's specific time limit for filing storm damage claims (which can range from a few months to a year after the event).",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  3
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "4. File the Claim",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Call your insurance company\u2019s claims hotline or submit a claim through your provider's mobile app or portal."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Provide the date of the storm and the preliminary notes from your inspection."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "5. Meet the Insurance Adjuster On-Site",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Schedule an insurance adjuster to inspect the roof in person."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Best practice: Have your roofing contractor present during the adjuster\u2019s inspection so they can point out all damaged areas together and ensure nothing is overlooked."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Avoid speculating, admitting pre-existing neglect, or guessing about the age of the wear-and-tear; stick strictly to the storm-related damage.",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  4
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "6. Review the Estimate & Complete Repairs",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "The insurance company will issue an initial scope of work and a payout (minus your deductible)."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "If the insurance estimate misses hidden damage or underprices local Utah labor/materials, your contractor can file a \"supplement\" request with the insurance company."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Once repairs are finished, submit the final paperwork to release any remaining withheld funds (depreciation, if you have an RCV policy)."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like more tailored guidance, tell me:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "What type of weather event caused the damage (hail, high wind, heavy snow/ice)?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Who your insurance provider is?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can share more specific tips for your situation."
      }
